**Update on `b07c6e3d`** — two fixture findings and one offer.

**1. A retyped `tool_use.input`.** `multi-turn-thinking-stream.json` recorded `"input":[]` where the request sends `"input":{}`. It is the trace of a decode/encode cycle — in PHP an empty array decoded from `{}` re-encodes as `[]` — not of the writer, which stores the body verbatim.

It hid because that fixture has `signature` but no `signature_v2`, so verification falls to the legacy path, where both sides are decoded and ksorted — and `json_decode('{}', true)` and `json_decode('[]', true)` are both the empty array. The lenient path cannot tell them apart.

My first attempt restored the whole pre-PR body, which was wrong: the re-record had also fixed a duplicated `thinking` block, and dropping that broke `Integration / Examples (replay)`. The pushed fix changes only the retyped value and recomputes the signature through `redactRequest()` itself. Also added a test that recomputes every interaction's signature from its own stored body.

**2. `brave.json`, left untouched on purpose.** 7 of its 15 interactions have a signature that does not reproduce their own body — exactly the 7 OpenAI requests carrying a body. I cannot establish which body is authoritative, and recomputing the hashes blind would only prove internal consistency, which is what this change argues against. Fix here, follow-up, or leave — your call.

**3. A data-loss bug that already affects `main`.** `HttpCassette::save()` encodes without `JSON_THROW_ON_ERROR`, so an unencodable body makes `json_encode()` return `false` and the file is overwritten with a bare newline. Measured on `main`, unpatched:

```
cassette after first record:     595 bytes
second record (resource body):   no error raised
cassette after:                  1 byte
```

Reachable via `Cartesia/CartesiaClient.php:95` and `ElevenLabs/ElevenLabsClient.php:71`, which pass an open `fopen()` handle as the body. `Test/Recording/Cassette.php` already guards against this and documents why. The commit also adds `JSON_PRESERVE_ZERO_FRACTION`, which `signature()` already hashes with.

That part is three lines plus tests and depends on nothing else here — happy to split it into its own PR so it can land while this one is in review.

CI is green except `Integration / Store / Milvus` (`Failed to connect to 127.0.0.1 port 19530`, 9 of 10 tests skipped); it fails identically on every push and this PR touches no store code.

